{"name":"Walrus moustache","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_%281%29.jpg/960px-Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_(1).jpg","caption":"Man with walrus moustache by T & J Holroyd (1)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["The walrus moustache is a thick, bushy moustache that falls over the mouth, named for walrus whiskers. It adds visual weight to the face and has long been associated with authority and ruggedness.","It was popular in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, declined in the 1920s and returned briefly in the 1960s. Wearers include Mark Twain, Friedrich Nietzsche, Theodore Roosevelt and, later, Sam Elliott."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["Growth is the main step: the hair is left to grow and droop over the lip, with occasional trimming. Results depend on upper-lip hair density, which is linked to androgen sensitivity and genetics."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Low risk; some wearers find food and drink collect in the hair. Patchy growth may limit the style."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_%281%29.jpg/960px-Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4c/Man_with_walrus_moustache_by_T_%26_J_Holroyd_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Man with walrus moustache by T & J Holroyd (1)","license":"Public domain","creator":"Thomas and James Holroyd","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Man_with_